d with the spacecraft.  No propulsion system GSE is required for this procedure.  The diagram below shows the overall test configuration for the propulsion system electrical integration:\n(Figure goes here)',6,33,'RUN'),(338,'Test and Measurement Equipment','Equipment used to perform quantitative measurements shall be Class 1 in accordance with JHU/APL Quality Management System (QMS) document [Q53-055] – JHU/APL Test and Measurement Equipment Practices and Procedures.  \n\nEquipment descriptions along with model, property number and calibration due date will be recorded within subsequent sections of this procedure.\n',7,33,'RUN'),(339,'Operational Constraints','SPP system environmental, EMC/EMI, and safety operational constraints during all phases of the integration of the propulsion system shall be adhered to by all personnel who are either directly or indirectly involved within this procedure.  The specific requirements and guidelines for these constraints are contained in the following documents: SPP Electromagnetic Environment Control Plan (7434-9040), SPP Contamination Control Plan (7434-9011) and SPP System Safety Program Plan (7434-9013).  In addition, specific operational constraints for the propulsion system are contained within the following sections.\n\nTest personnel will be trained and certified in appropriate ESD, cleanroom, security and safety procedures, as applicable.  Only personnel that have received appropriate training and who hold necessary certifications will be allowed to perform activities involving the spacecraft and its components.  \n',8,33,'RUN'),(340,'Propulsion System Contamination Constraints','To avoid contamination of the system, the upstream valve must always be opened prior to the downstream valve.',9,33,'RUN'),(341,'Propulsion System Safety and Handling Constraints','There are no safety constraints concerning the operations in this procedure, and this procedure is not a hazardous operation.\n\nThere are several handling constraints for the propulsion system in this procedure.  When actually firing thrusters, the upstream valve must always be opened prior to the downstream valve.  Thruster covers must also be removed prior to actually firing thrusters.\n',10,33,'RUN'),(342,'Propulsion System Limited Life Items','TBD',11,33,'RUN'),(343,'Scope','This document applies to the electrical integration of the propulsion system to the SPP spacecraft.  This procedure contains supplementary documentation, test equipment, and detailed procedural steps needed for initial integration.  The spacecraft is delivered to I&T with the propulsion system already mechanically installed. ',1,34,'ORIGINAL'),(344,'Application Documents and Drawings','SPP Spacecraft Documents: \n7434-7300 	SPP Systems Integration and Test Plan\n7434-9003	SPP Performance Assurance Implementation Plan (PAIP)\n7434-9039 	SPP Environmental Design and Test Requirements Document (EDTRD)\n7434-9040 	SPP Electromagnetic Environment Control Plan (EMECP)\n7434-9011 	SPP Contamination Control Plan (CPP)\n7434-9013	SPP System Safety Program Plan (SPP)	\n7434-9077	SPP Spacecraft Electrical ICD\n7434-9531	SPP Spacecraft Mechanical ICD\n\nJHU/APL Quality Documents:\nQL2-060	Electrostatic Discharge Control \nQF3-055	Test and Measurement Equipment Practices and Procedures                                         \nSD-QP-515	Contamination Control Process (w/ sub documents SD-WI-515-01 thru -06)\nSD-QP-625	Inspection Activities & Mandatory Inspections (w/ sub documents SD-WI-625-01 and -02)\nSD-QP-301	System Safety Policy\nSD-WI-301-01	Development of Hazardous Procedures\nSD-QP-105	Anomaly, Problem and Failure Reporting Process\nSD-QP-505	Requirements and Best Practices for Handling Flight Hardware\nSD-WI-740-01	Mechanical Fastener Torque Procedure and Torque Values\n\nPropulsion Documents/ Drawings: \n7434-8001	Main Spacecraft Harness\n42288			Aerojet Electrical ICD\n42290			Aerojet Mechanical ICD\n',2,34,'ORIGINAL'),(345,'Procedure Summary','(Figures g